    {
      "type": "intuition",
      "term": "optics-m4-phase-difference-e-o",
      "title": "Why the e-ray and o-ray drift out of step",
      "meaning": "The e-ray and o-ray travel at different speeds, so they leave the crystal out of step.",
      "points": [
        "<b>Start</b>: both waves are in phase at the front face.",
        "<b>Speeds</b>: the two rays have different velocities in the crystal.",
        "<b>Result</b>: an optical path difference \\((\\mu_o-\\mu_e)d\\) after thickness d."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "info",
      "label": "04 · §20.17 Definition",
      "title": "What is the phase difference between e- and o-rays?",
      "defines": [
        "optics-m4-phase-difference-e-o"
      ],
      "body": "The phase difference between e- and o-rays is \\(2\\pi/\\lambda\\) times their optical path difference, \\((\\mu_o-\\mu_e)d\\).",
      "points": [
        "Optical path difference: \\((\\mu_o-\\mu_e)d\\) (Eq. 20.35).",
        "Phase difference: \\(\\delta = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}(\\mu_o-\\mu_e)d\\) (Eq. 20.36).",
        "<b>No fringes</b>: e- and o-waves are polarized at right angles.",
        "They combine into a polarization state, not an interference pattern."
      ],
      "tex": "\\delta = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}(\\mu_o-\\mu_e)\\,d",
      "art": {
